---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import InfiniteSlider from '../components/InfiniteSlider.astro';
import StockTicker from '../components/StockTicker.astro';
import Carousel from '../components/Carousel.astro';
import SeeMoreInfo from '../components/SeeMoreInfo.astro';
const { services = "/services"} = Astro.props;

import mainImage04 from '../assets/images/background/04star-bottle-1.webp';

const Name = "Stellarwaves-5";
---
<Layout title={Name}>
    <SeeMoreInfo titleColum2 = "HOW TO SEND YOUR MESSAGE INTO SPACE", titleColum3 = "HOW WILL YOUR MESSAGE BE LAUNCHED INTO SPACE?">
        <p slot="column2">
            Sending your message into space is no longer just Science Fiction; today you can do it from this page with a beam of light pointing toward the sky. You just need to follow the instructions. 
            <a class="blink-02" href={services}>To send your message, just click here. </a> 
            We have always Middle Column) looked at the sky with curiosity. Today, with light technology, you can send your ideas, emotions, or whatever comes to mind, directly to the universe. This page gives you the chance to
            join this cosmic adventure. Your message is going to shoot out like a beam of light, toward the sky.
            Humanity learned to look at the sky with awe, always searching for answers among the stars. Today, thanks to light, we can send our own words to the universe. This page invites you to be part of this cosmic journey.
        </p>

        <p slot="column3">
            Your message will be launched into space with a beam of light using binary code, a <b>universal language</b> used by <b>NASA and most space agencies on our planet to send messages into space</b>. Although it is not a "language" in the human sense, its simplicity makes it a powerful tool for interstellar communication. All modern electronics, from computers to radio telescopes, are built on the foundation of the binary system. Spacecraft, satellites, and NASA ground stations are no exception.
            Not only messages, but also communication with spacecraft through NASA's Deep Space Network (DSN) are carried out via radio waves. These radio waves carry binary-encoded information, which is then decoded by computers on Earth. The process is similar to that of your mobile phone, and to the one we use to send messages into space, but on a much larger scale.
            In short, binary code is not only for sending messages, but is also used by NASA and all space agencies as an intrinsic language of digital technology that makes space exploration possible.
        </p>
    </SeeMoreInfo>
</Layout>
<style>
    body{
        /* background-image: url(../assets/images/background/star-bottle-4-blu.webp) center / cover no-repeat; */
        background: center / 100% 100% no-repeat url(../assets/images/background/new/saturn-farewell.webp);
    }
